menu "DGX"

config DGX_ENABLE_SPI
    bool "Enable SPI transport backend"
    default n

config DGX_ENABLE_I2C
    bool "Enable I2C transport backend"
    default n

config DGX_ENABLE_P8
    bool "Enable 8-bit parallel transport backend"
    depends on SOC_LCD_I80_SUPPORTED
    default n

config DGX_ENABLE_DSI
    bool "Enable MIPI-DSI transport backend"
    depends on SOC_MIPI_DSI_SUPPORTED
    default n

config DGX_DSI_PRIVATE_DMA2D
    bool "Drive the 2D DMA of a DSI display through the private interface of IDF"
    depends on DGX_ENABLE_DSI && SOC_DMA2D_SUPPORTED
    default y
    help
        An escape hatch, not a setting. The bus moves large pieces of pixels
        by the 2D DMA of the chip: a part of a 16-bit virtual screen put to
        the display and, with two frame buffers, what one of them lacks of
        the other. The 2D DMA can exchange the two bytes of a pixel on the
        way, which only the private interface of IDF, esp_private/dma2d.h,
        gives; the bus also sets one field of a register directly, since IDF
        6.0.3 and later do not set it for 16-bit pixels. A private interface
        may change in any release of IDF.

        Leave it on. Turn it off only when an IDF no longer builds this code:
        the processor then moves the pieces, several times slower, and
        everything else works as before. The fill of a rectangle and the mix
        of two colors by the pixel processing accelerator use a public
        interface and do not depend on this option.

config DGX_DSI_INDEXED_BAND_KB
    int "Band of an 8-bit screen on its way to a DSI display, KB"
    depends on DGX_DSI_PRIVATE_DMA2D
    range 8 256
    default 64
    help
        An 8-bit screen put to a DSI display through a color table is made
        into rows of the panel by the processor, a band at a time, while the
        2D DMA moves the band before it into the frame buffer. There are two
        bands, in internal RAM, taken for the time of the call. Larger bands
        are faster: on a 1280x800 screen a full picture took 11.1 ms with 32
        KB, 9.9 with 64 and 9.3 with 100, and an overlay layer is mixed in
        faster too. Where internal RAM has no room for two bands of this
        size, smaller ones are tried, down to a quarter of it.

